Replicate the Chat System wiki overhaul pattern across the rest of the Fusion Studio wiki and codebase domains.

## What Was Done

The old Chat System wiki lived under `001-Workspaces_And_Views/003-Chat_System/` as a view-adjacent architecture subtree. That shape made future sessions treat chat like a view feature even though it crosses persistence, runtime, harness adapters, WebSocket protocol, renderer state, UI chrome, metadata, clipboard, testing, and operations.

Chat was promoted into its own top-level wiki domain:

```text
ai/<machine>/Wiki/007-Chat_System/
```

The new domain was split into focused pages:

- overview
- identity and persistence
- harness and event flow
- rendering and lifecycle
- chat UI
- user metadata
- text and media payloads
- testing and operations
- decisions
- lessons
- vision
- changelog
- structure
- runtime model

The old Workspaces chat pages were converted into compatibility pointers so stale duplicate guidance is not maintained in two places.

Supporting rule pages were also added for recurring planning defaults:

- `005-Enforcement/003-Chat_Styling_And_Workspace_CSS/`
- `005-Enforcement/004-Stub_And_Planning_Defaults/`
- `002-System_Tools/003-Clipboard_History/`
- `001-Project/024-Issue_Viewer_Ticket_Authoring/`

`AGENTS.md` and the assistant reply chrome spec were updated to point future sessions at the new Chat System source of truth.

## Why It Was Needed

Planning the assistant reply chrome feature required too much conversational clarification because several durable rules were implicit or scattered:

- Chat ID means SQLite `exchanges.id`, not `threadId`.
- Stop/interrupted turns must persist through the same exchange-save path as normal completion.
- Reply chrome must wait for saved exchange acknowledgement.
- Reply text should come from finalized `Message.segments`, not DOM scraping.
- Bookmark and note metadata are post-save exchange metadata, not turn-end collectors.
- Clipboard writes should use managed clipboard history.
- Chat styling comes from workspace/system CSS variables, not hardcoded frontend values.
- Current harness flow uses OpenCode -> canonical events -> universal event bus, not old Kimi raw wire protocol.
- Browser Playwright, Electron Playwright, and Fusion Home restart serve different verification needs.

Those details should be discoverable from wiki articles before a feature-planning session starts.

## Pattern To Replicate

For each major system domain:

1. Promote it to a top-level wiki domain if it crosses multiple code layers.
2. Split documentation by durable responsibility instead of one long architecture page.
3. Preserve cross-cutting pages: Decisions, Lessons, Vision, Changelog, Structure, and Runtime/Operations when relevant.
4. Move old content into the new tree and convert old pages into compatibility pointers.
5. Update `AGENTS.md`, specs, and source-of-truth references so future sessions start in the new tree.
6. Add small enforcement/tooling pages when repeated planning defaults are not domain-specific.
7. Run a link check after migration.

## Candidate Domains

- Workspaces and Views
- Server and Runtime
- Automation and Agents
- Integrations and Tools
- System Tools
- Enforcement
- Operations
- Wiki View
- Issues Viewer
- Browser
- System Manager

## Acceptance Criteria

- Each major domain has a clear top-level wiki entry or an explicit reason it remains nested.
- Each major domain has Decisions, Lessons, Vision, Changelog, and Structure pages where useful.
- Old migrated pages are compatibility pointers, not parallel sources of truth.
- Root wiki navigation reflects durable system domains.
- `AGENTS.md` and active specs point to current wiki source-of-truth paths.
- Repeated planning defaults are documented once and linked from affected domains.
- A link-existence check passes after each domain migration.
